<article class="env-lemma" id="0F70">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0F70">Lemma <span data-tag="0F70">59.70.4</span>.</a> Let $j : U \to X$ be an open immersion of schemes. For any abelian sheaf $\mathcal{F}$ on $U_{\acute{e}tale}$, the adjunction mappings $j^{-1}j_*\mathcal{F} \to \mathcal{F}$ and $\mathcal{F} \to j^{-1}j_!\mathcal{F}$ are isomorphisms. In fact, $j_!\mathcal{F}$ is the unique abelian sheaf on $X_{\acute{e}tale}$ whose restriction to $U$ is $\mathcal{F}$ and whose stalks at geometric points of $X \setminus U$ are zero. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      We encourage the reader to prove the first statement by working through the definitions, but here we just use that it is a special case of the very general Modules on Sites, Lemma <a href="/tag/0F6Z" data-tag="0F6Z">18.19.8</a>. For the second statement, observe that if $\mathcal{G}$ is an abelian sheaf on $X_{\acute{e}tale}$ whose restriction to $U$ is $\mathcal{F}$, then we obtain by adjointness a map $j_!\mathcal{F} \to \mathcal{G}$. This map is then an isomorphism at stalks of geometric points of $U$ by Proposition <a href="/tag/03S5" data-tag="03S5">59.70.3</a>. Thus if $\mathcal{G}$ has vanishing stalks at geometric points of $X \setminus U$, then $j_!\mathcal{F} \to \mathcal{G}$ is an isomorphism by Theorem <a href="/tag/03PU" data-tag="03PU">59.29.10</a>. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>